{
    // Knip — unused files / exports / dependencies.
    //
    // Run it with `npm run knip`. It IS wired into CI (`ci.yml`), which it was
    // not until now, and the two reasons it was held back are addressed here
    // rather than waived:
    //
    //   - "an `export` keyword that is redundant but harmless" — that is real,
    //     and `ignoreExportsUsedInFile` below is the setting for exactly it, so
    //     those stop being reported at all instead of being triaged by hand
    //     every sweep.
    //   - "a meaningful share is a false positive" — each remaining one is now
    //     named in `ignoreDependencies` / `ignoreBinaries` with the reason,
    //     so the report is empty when the repo is clean.
    //
    // That is the bargain a gate requires: a check that is red by default
    // teaches people to ignore it. Before it went into CI it had been failing
    // on master with nothing watching, which is the same failure in the other
    // direction. If this starts reporting noise again, fix the config or the
    // code — do not take it back out of CI and leave it red.
    //
    // ⚠️ VERIFY A KNIP CHANGE IN A CLEAN CLONE, NOT IN A DIRTY WORKING TREE OR
    // A GIT WORKTREE. This config's `apps/audio-rendition` block was landed
    // twice: the first attempt passed `npm run knip` locally and failed in CI
    // on two `Remove redundant entry pattern` hints, and the fix for THAT
    // reported an unused `esbuild` devDependency locally while being correct in
    // CI — the two environments disagreed in both directions on the same
    // commit. A `git clone` of the repo into a scratch directory followed by
    // `npm ci` reproduced CI exactly, every time; the working tree did not,
    // including immediately after its own clean `npm ci`. The cause was not
    // found (it is not build state, not a stale node_modules, not the dot
    // directory in the worktree path — each was tested and ruled out), so
    // treat this as a standing caveat rather than something fixed: knip's
    // entry auto-detection is the part that diverges, so any change to an
    // `entry` or `project` pattern here needs the clean-clone check before it
    // is believed. Everything else in CI (typecheck, lint, test, build) agreed
    // between the two.
    //
    // Known blind spot, important when reading a report: knip resolves imports
    // through node_modules, so it cannot follow this repo's tsconfig path
    // aliases (`shared/*` -> `packages/shared/*`, `@antiphony/core/*` ->
    // `packages/core/*`). Anything reached only through an alias looks unused
    // to it. That is also why it could not see that `packages/core/ports/
    // auth-port.ts` was dead — a barrel re-export in `index.ts` made the file
    // reachable from an entry point even though nothing imported the barrel.
    "$schema": "https://unpkg.com/knip@6/schema.json",

    "workspaces": {
        // The repo root is a workspace to knip too. Its source is `scripts/`
        // and `eslint-rules/`, both run from root npm scripts rather than
        // imported by anything — without these entries `validate-lexicons.mjs`
        // reads as a dead file and its only dependency (`@atproto/lex-document`)
        // as an unused devDependency.
        //
        // `eslint-rules/` is DECLARED here rather than ignored. It used to sit
        // under a blanket `"ignore": ["eslint-rules/**"]`, with the directory
        // kept in `project` purely so that ignore still matched something —
        // which knip eventually reported as redundant config. Naming the two
        // real entry shapes is both more honest and stricter: `index.mjs` is
        // imported by each workspace's eslint.config.mjs across a boundary knip
        // does not follow, and the RuleTester suites run under `node --test`
        // via the root `test:eslint-rules` script. An unreferenced rule file
        // that is in neither now reports as dead, which the blanket ignore
        // could never do.
        ".": {
            "entry": ["scripts/*.mjs", "eslint-rules/index.mjs", "eslint-rules/*.test.mjs"],
            "project": ["scripts/**/*.mjs", "eslint-rules/**/*.mjs"]
        },
        "apps/core-api": {
            // `src/worker.ts` is the only runtime entry, and knip finds it
            // itself from `main` in apps/core-api/wrangler.jsonc — listing it
            // here would be redundant and knip says so.
            //
            // `src/native.ts` used to be listed beside `scripts/*.ts`, to keep
            // the Firebase adapters that only the Node runtime could reach from
            // reading as dead. That file was deleted with Firebase and the Node
            // runtime in 1b023cd, so the pattern had matched nothing since —
            // knip reported it as an unmatched entry pattern, and that report
            // is what surfaced it.
            "entry": ["scripts/*.ts"],
            "project": ["src/**/*.ts", "scripts/**/*.ts"]
        },
        "apps/audio-rendition": {
            // No `entry` here on purpose. It used to name `src/index.ts` and
            // `esbuild.config.mjs`, and knip reports BOTH as redundant: it
            // already finds the first through `main` in this workspace's
            // package.json (mapped back through tsconfig) and the second
            // through the `build` script that runs it. Stating them again
            // added nothing, and as redundant config it fails the gate.
            "project": ["src/**/*.ts"]
        },
        "apps/reference": {
            "project": ["src/**/*.{ts,tsx}"]
        },
        "apps/docs": {
            // `.mdx` is listed because knip 6 compiles it and reports a hint
            // when a workspace has `.mdx` files outside `project`.
            // `src/content/docs/index.mdx` is one, so leaving it out fails the gate.
            "project": ["src/**/*.{ts,astro,mdx}"]
        },
        "packages/core": {
            "entry": ["index.ts"],
            "project": ["**/*.ts"]
        },
        "packages/shared": {
            // Entry points come from the `exports` map in package.json, which
            // knip reads on its own — every published subpath counts as one.
            "project": ["**/*.ts"]
        },
        "packages/capture-kit": {
            // Same as shared: `exports` supplies the entry points. `.tsx` is in
            // `project` because AudioPlayer is a component — without it knip
            // reports the file as unused and `react` as an unused dependency.
            "project": ["**/*.{ts,tsx}"]
        },
        "packages/tokens": {
            "project": ["**/*.ts"]
        }
    },

    // These are NOT unused. Each is a knip resolution gap, verified by hand:
    //
    //   @antiphony/shared        imported 14x by core-api and 18x by
    //                            packages/core, but via the `shared/*` tsconfig
    //                            path alias rather than the package specifier,
    //                            which knip does not follow.
    //   @antiphony/tokens        imported into apps/docs/src/styles/brand.css
    //                            via CSS @import (tokens.css); knip reads JS
    //                            imports only.
    //   @fontsource-variable/*   referenced from `apps/docs/astro.config.mjs`
    //                            in a Vite `optimizeDeps` string array and from
    //                            `src/styles/brand.css`; knip reads neither.
    //
    // `sharp` was here too (Astro's image service loads it by name). knip 6
    // follows that through its Astro plugin, and listing it here is now
    // redundant config, so it was removed in the knip 6 upgrade.
    //
    // Re-verify before deleting any of these, don't just trust the report.
    "ignoreDependencies": [
        "@antiphony/shared",
        "@antiphony/tokens",
        "@fontsource-variable/inter",
        "@fontsource-variable/sora",
        "@fontsource-variable/jetbrains-mono"
    ],

    // `ffmpeg` is a system binary, not an npm package. apps/audio-rendition
    // spawns it (`src/rendition.ts`), and its Dockerfile installs it with apt
    // and asserts it is on PATH. knip 6 started reporting spawned binaries it
    // cannot find in any package.json.
    "ignoreBinaries": ["ffmpeg"],

    // An export used only inside its own file is a redundant `export` keyword,
    // not dead code — the module still needs the binding. That distinction was
    // the single biggest source of noise here (eleven of the thirteen exports
    // reported when this went into CI), and triaging it by hand every sweep is
    // what made the report feel not worth gating on. Knip has a setting for
    // exactly this case; use it instead of deleting `export` keywords one at a
    // time, and note that genuinely dead code is still reported — the sweep
    // that turned this on also found `neonConnectionString`, unreferenced since
    // the commit that introduced it, and deleted it.
    "ignoreExportsUsedInFile": true,

    // `types` is off because it reports ~21 exported interfaces that are the
    // declared shape of a module's own contract — `AudioPostDependencies`,
    // `RateLimitOptions`, `ServiceApp` and friends. Exporting those is the
    // point of the ports pattern, not an oversight, and leaving the check on
    // buries the findings that do matter.
    "rules": {
        "types": "off",

        // `duplicates` is off because this repo aliases deliberately, and the
        // published contract is where it does it: `ProcessingViewSchema` IS
        // `ProcessingStageMapSchema`, and the separate name is the point — one
        // is the structural schema, the other is the named thing the hydrated
        // view promises, and `types/audio.ts` composes the view name. Both are
        // genuinely used, so there is nothing to remove; the rule simply cannot
        // tell a semantic alias in a contract from an accident.
        //
        // Tried first and did not work: knip's `tags` (`@knipAlias` on the
        // export) filters unused-export detection but not this rule, so the
        // finding survived it. If a narrower mechanism appears, prefer it —
        // turning the rule off does mean an ACCIDENTAL duplicate now goes
        // unreported, which is a real cost. The one that existed when this
        // landed, `r2BlobStore|blobStore` in apps/audio-rendition, was fixed
        // properly instead (the internal one is no longer exported) rather than
        // swept under this switch.
        "duplicates": "off"
    }
}
